  graphExplanationText: "This is the most endothermic reaction in the lab: 340.1 kilojoules per mole of reaction have to be put in, which is why the green crystals need sustained strong heating before the reddish-brown iron oxide appears. The barrier of 365 kilojoules per mole sits only just above that, so like the limestone this is a temperature-driven decomposition rather than a kinetically hindered one. What makes it possible at all is the entropy: plus 377.4 joules per mole per kelvin, the largest change of any reaction here, because two moles of ordered solid break apart into a solid plus two different gases, sulfur dioxide and sulfur trioxide. Those two escaping gases are the whole reason it works. The crossover temperature comes out around 628 degrees Celsius, which matches how hot you actually have to get iron sulfate before it will decompose."
